Translation patch: <EdwardsPoint as VartimeMultiscalarMul>::optional_multiscalar_mul (src/edwards.rs)

Change: As in edwards-multiscalar_mul.patch: the `size_hint` checks become `assert_eq!` on the
lengths of the collected `Vec`s, and `size = scalars.len()` (instead of the size hint) picks
Straus (`size < 190`) or Pippenger.

Why: the Aeneas Lean library models only `Iterator::next`; the provided
methods (`map`, `zip`, `fold`, `filter`, `collect`, `rev`/`skip` on adapters, ...)
are missing (AeneasVerif/aeneas#1057), so the original does not build in Lean.
`size_hint` and `by_ref` are not modelled, and no rewrite can call them. Aeneas also cannot extract a loop that calls `next()` directly on the
`IntoIter` of a generic `IntoIterator` parameter ("Could not find: type_var_id ..
from ExtractBase.Item"), so the consuming loop lives in a nested helper function
generic over `It: Iterator`, called with `x.into_iter()`.

Behaviour: **not identical, same result for valid inputs.** The original panics unless
both size hints are exact and equal; the patch panics iff the actual lengths differ. The
algorithm choice uses the actual length (equal to the hint for exact-size iterators); both
algorithms compute the same point. Inputs are now read eagerly, scalars before points.

Remove this patch when Aeneas translates the original code: delete the file,
run ./scripts/aeneas-translate.sh and `lake build`, and check both succeed.

diff --git a/curve25519-dalek/src/edwards.rs b/curve25519-dalek/src/edwards.rs
index 1120e8f..c1ada07 100644
--- a/curve25519-dalek/src/edwards.rs
+++ b/curve25519-dalek/src/edwards.rs
@@ -1002,39 +1002,40 @@ impl VartimeMultiscalarMul for EdwardsPoint {
     fn optional_multiscalar_mul<I, J>(scalars: I, points: J) -> Option<EdwardsPoint>
     where
         I: IntoIterator,
         I::Item: Borrow<Scalar>,
         J: IntoIterator<Item = Option<EdwardsPoint>>,
     {
-        // Sanity-check lengths of input iterators
-        let mut scalars = scalars.into_iter();
-        let mut points = points.into_iter();
+        fn collect<It: Iterator>(mut it: It) -> Vec<It::Item> {
+            let mut out = Vec::new();
+            while let Some(x) = it.next() {
+                out.push(x);
+            }
+            out
+        }
 
-        // Lower and upper bounds on iterators
-        let (s_lo, s_hi) = scalars.by_ref().size_hint();
-        let (p_lo, p_hi) = points.by_ref().size_hint();
+        // Sanity-check lengths of input iterators
+        let scalars = collect(scalars.into_iter());
+        let points = collect(points.into_iter());
 
-        // They should all be equal
-        assert_eq!(s_lo, p_lo);
-        assert_eq!(s_hi, Some(s_lo));
-        assert_eq!(p_hi, Some(p_lo));
+        // They should be equal
+        assert_eq!(scalars.len(), points.len());
 
-        // Now we know there's a single size.
-        // Use this as the hint to decide which algorithm to use.
-        let size = s_lo;
+        // Use the size to decide which algorithm to use.
+        let size = scalars.len();
 
         if size < 190 {
             crate::backend::straus_optional_multiscalar_mul(scalars, points)
         } else {
             crate::backend::pippenger_optional_multiscalar_mul(scalars, points)
         }
     }
 }
 
 /// Precomputation for variable-time multiscalar multiplication with `EdwardsPoint`s.
 // This wraps the inner implementation in a facade type so that we can
 // decouple stability of the inner type from the stability of the
 // outer type.
 #[cfg(feature = "alloc")]
 pub struct VartimeEdwardsPrecomputation(crate::backend::VartimePrecomputedStraus);
 
